Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) with multiple emission centers are newly emerging as ratiometric sensors owing to their high sensitivity and high selectivity toward a wide range of targeted functional species. Energy transfer between the light-absorbing group and emission centers and between different emission centers is the key to rationally design and synthesize MOF-based ratiometric sensors. A good match between the energy levels of the lightabsorbing groups and emission centers is the prerequisite for MOF-based sensors to exhibit multiple emissions, and a good match of the MOF-based sensors and those of the targeted species can increase the sensitivity and selectivity, but this match is highly challenging to obtain via synthesis. MOFs with multiple emission centers can be produced by functionalizing MOFs with multiple lanthanide centers, organic luminophores, dyes, carbon dots, and other such emissive groups. In this progress report, recent advances in the strategies for synthesizing MOFs with multiple emission centers and their applications for ratiometric sensing of solution conditions, including the pH value, and ion, organic molecule, and biomolecule concentrations, are summarized, as are the related sensing mechanisms.
## 1. Introduction
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), as a new class of porous crystalline materials formed using coordination bonds between metal nodes and organic linkers, have attracted great interest in material science due to their facile synthesis routes and diverse functionalities.[1–5] Their porous nature, functional metal nodes, and organic linkers endow them with great potentials for gas storage and separation, catalysis, biomedicine, proton conductivity, magnetism, luminescence, and other fields.[6–12]
The application of MOFs as sensing materials is one of their most important applications. Both metal ions and organic linkers of MOFs are emission centers if they are well designed. As luminescent or fluorescent sensors for specific targeted species, MOFs have the following advantages: i) the structural and functional diversity and tunability of MOFs match almost all the types of targeted species such as cations, anions, organic molecules, and even nanoparticles[13] via different host–guest interaction mechanisms;[14] ii) the emission intensities can correlate well with the concentrations of the targeted species in practical ranges; and iii) the preparation of MOF-based sensors and the equipment to measure the luminescence intensities are facile, inexpensive, and easy-to-operate in comparison with the methods of gas chromatography mass spectrometry (GC/MS) or high-performance liquid chromatography (HPLC), which can provide reliable results but usually require expensive instruments and the usage of large amounts of solvents.
For the sensing functions of MOF-based sensors, there are two ways that the luminescent signals change upon interaction with a guest species: i) a change in the wavelength of the emission peaks could be found in transition metal–based MOFs whose emissions are usually from a charge transfer transition between metal centers and ligands or intramolecular electron transfer; and ii) a change in the luminescent intensities could be monitored without a change in the wavelength, which is usually found in lanthanide-based MOFs because the f–f electron transitions of lanthanide centers are not influenced by external perturbations.[11,12,15] The mechanism behind these luminescence signal changes can be classified as photoelectron transfer (PET), resonant energy transfer (FRET), or competitive absorption.[15] A comprehensive understanding of the mechanism in a practical system is of great importance for the rational design and synthesis of high-performance MOF-based sensors.
For MOFs with one type of emission center, the monochromatic luminescence/fluorescence signal change is good enough for the quantitative analysis of the analyte; however, the sensitivity, selectivity, and detection limit are still not comparable to those of the HPLC or GC/MS techniques mentioned above due to the influence of other external conditions.[15] To improve the high sensitivity, high selectivity, and low detection limit of the sensing performance, rational design and synthesis of ratiometric sensors based on MOFs with multiple emission centers has been recently demonstrated as a powerful strategy for solving the problems mentioned above because the self-calibrating or self-referencing mechanism from different but correlated emission centers can minimize the external influence.[16] The self-calibrating ability by comparison of the emission intensities from different centers can significantly enhance the selectivity and sensitivity if the host (MOF) and guest (analyte) can be well matched. In addition, ratiometric luminescent or fluorescent sensors with strong visible color changes are more sensitive and beneficial for the on-site detection by the naked eyes. There are also kinds of ratiometric sensors based on nonMOF materials which were well reviewed, for example, the nanoscale dual-emitting sensors for temperature[17] and the fluorophores with emissions from two excited-states as ratiometric sensors,[18] but these types of ratiometric sensors are out of the scope of this progress report.
A comprehensive database of MOF-based sensors in which they are categorized by their target analytes has been established and reviewed recently.[11,12,15,16,19] However, MOF-based ratiometric sensors were not included. In this progress report, we summarize both the synthetic strategies for MOF-based ratiometric sensors with multiple emission centers and their sensing applications for a variety of targeted species, especially environmental and biotic species.
## 2. Strategies for the Design and Synthesis of MOF-Based Ratiometric Sensors
To rationally design and synthesize MOF-based ratiometric sensors, several strategies have been developed. The introduction of dual or multiple emission centers, the construction of an effective energy transfer pathway between these centers, and the optimization of host–guest interactions between MOFs and analytes are key to realize such applications. In addition, chemically tuning the relative amount of the emission centers, the space and distance between the emission centers, and the distribution of the emission centers are important in the synthesis route to improve the sensitivity and reduce detection limit of the sensor. Based on the emission centers, MOF-based ratiometric sensors can be classified into i) MOFs with dual emission (from both the metal center and the organic ligand, or from different metal centers, or from different organic ligands); and ii) MOF-based hybrids that incorporate other types of emission centers such as dyes, carbon dots (CDs), and luminescent complexes.
## 2.1. MOFs with Two Emission Centers
Excellent design of organic linkers with appropriate energy levels and metal centers results in singular MOFs that exhibit dual emission from bimetallic centers, one metal center with one organic center or two organic emission centers to function as ratiometric sensors.
